---
import DocGuide from '../../layouts/DocGuide.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../data/urls';
import { sourceUrl } from '../../data/source';
const sections: [string, string][] = [['startup','01 · Startup'],['ownership','02 · Responsibility map'],['theming','03 · Theme authority'],['menus','04 · Compatibility adapters'],['verify','05 · Inspect and recover']];
const ownership = [
  ['Bar, dock, launcher, widgets', 'Lucent desktop', 'Components describe trees; the framework lays out, animates and renders Vulkan buffers.'],
  ['Desktop wallpaper', 'Lucent background surface', 'WallpaperPort → LucentWallpaper persists the selected file. AssetPort → NativeAssets decodes it off the UI thread.'],
  ['Notifications, history, DND', 'Lucent', 'FreedesktopNotifications owns the D-Bus name. Native components render toasts and history; notification shortcuts route here.'],
  ['Light/dark and shell appearance', 'Lucent tokens', 'design/tokens.json → compiled theme → components. DesktopSettings.light chooses the mode.'],
  ['Application colors and mode', 'Lucent export → applications', 'ThemePort → LucentTheme → theme.py. Omarchy is a headless template exporter, never the theme authority.'],
  ['Lock and login', 'Lucent clients + PAM / greetd', 'Secure session-lock surfaces for locking; a separate greeter process for login. Both share the selected wallpaper and mode.'],
  ['Permission prompts', 'Lucent UI + libpolkit-agent', 'A standard registered polkit agent creates a native authorization client. The trusted library/helper performs authentication.'],
  ['Idle and sleep', 'swayidle + Lucent lock adapter', 'Headless compositor/logind integration. Stay-awake suppresses idle locking, while sleep and explicit lock handlers remain.'],
  ['Lock recovery', 'Independent hyprlock', 'Emergency fallback if the native locker fails. Uses exported Lucent tokens and wallpaper; no stock shell is started.'],
  ['Keybinding and nested menus', 'Lucent picker + installed definitions', 'Omarchy supplies binding/action data. lucent-menu renders the picker with the framework and returns the original selected value.'],
  ['Audio, display and power menus', 'Lucent picker + system commands', 'Typed picker requests from compatibility adapters. pactl/wpctl, Hyprland and powerprofilesctl perform operations.'],
  ['Network and Bluetooth', 'Native selection + system tools', 'Saved connections and known devices have native selectors. New Wi-Fi setup uses nmtui; pairing uses bluetoothctl’s agent in a terminal. These are separate system applications.'],
  ['Clipboard and emoji', 'Lucent picker + headless capture', 'Private bounded history via wl-paste; sensitivity hints are honored. Emoji data is read from the installed distribution. Selection copies/pastes after releasing focus.'],
  ['Volume and brightness feedback', 'Lucent OSD', 'Existing backend shortcuts translate to transient native feedback, with no notification history entry or keyboard grab.'],
  ['Tiling, splits, focus, outputs', 'Hyprland', 'The compositor manages application windows. Lucent sends requests through its compositor adapter.'],
  ['Window corners and gaps', 'Lucent tokens → Hyprland', 'Generated user configuration supplies radius and spacing defaults. Fullscreen and explicit app rules can override them.'],
  ['Screenshot region selection', 'Installed capture tools', 'Existing bindings retain grim/slurp and capture commands. Lucent does not implement a replacement region picker.'],
  ['Updates, packages, storage', 'Arch / Omarchy backends', 'Existing administrative commands remain available. Replacing the shell does not replace the distribution.'],
  ['Tray and optional stock plugins', 'Not implemented', 'There is no native StatusNotifier watcher or stock QML plugin host. Unsupported plugin IPC fails instead of starting another shell.'],
];
---
<DocGuide chapter="system" title="Lucent and system ownership" heading="One visual shell." accent="Explicit system adapters." description="Lucent owns shell presentation and theme tokens. System services and selected Omarchy commands provide backend behavior." sections={sections} audience="Desktop integration guide">
  <section id="startup"><span class="eyebrow">01 / WHAT STARTS</span><h2>Lucent starts with the session.</h2>
    <p>The user Hyprland configuration prepends Lucent’s compatibility commands to PATH and starts <code>lucent.service</code>. The installed Omarchy startup callback still invokes <code>omarchy-launch-shell</code>, but its user-level adapter starts the same Lucent service. Systemd keeps one instance. Packaged files are untouched.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={`Hyprland login
  → user PATH / autostart
  → lucent.service → session.py → lucent-desktop
       ├─ background / bar / dock / widgets / notifications / OSD
       ├─ lucent-polkit.service → libpolkit-agent + native dialog
       ├─ lucent-idle.service → swayidle → secure lock
       └─ lucent-clipboard.service → wl-paste → private history

Permission request → polkit → lucent-polkit → SessionScreen
Lock request       → lock.py → lucent-lock → ext-session-lock
Theme selection    → ThemePort → LucentTheme → app exports`} label="Active native-shell integration" />
    <p>Activation stops only the current user’s installed stock shell and its restart supervisor, after checking native readiness. It refuses to interrupt a secure lock. A desktop failure restarts Lucent; it does not silently switch visual shells. Explicit rollback restores stock startup. Read <a href={sourceUrl('scripts/lucent-session.py')}>the supervisor</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('scripts/lucent-setup.py')}>the installer</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">02 / CURRENT RESPONSIBILITIES</span><h2>Rendering and behavior<br />have separate owners.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Owner</th><th>Implementation and limits</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ownership.map(([feature, owner, behavior]) => <tr><td>{feature}</td><td>{owner}</td><td>{behavior}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>This is not a claim of full stock-plugin parity. Wi-Fi QR, custom stock plugins and system-tray hosting remain missing; the complete native control center is still future work. Hardware-specific Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, battery, brightness, sleep and physical display behavior need hardware validation. A VM cannot establish those results.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="theming"><span class="eyebrow">03 / ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH</span><h2>The direction is outward.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={`design/tokens.json
  ├─ primitive palette / spacing / type / motion
  └─ semantic roles → component recipes
       ├─ generated Rust → Lucent clients
       ├─ generated CSS → documentation/design system
       ├─ window rules → Hyprland
       └─ light/dark export → application configuration
            ├─ Omarchy templates (headless, background preserved)
            ├─ GTK named colors + desktop color-scheme
            ├─ Foot / tmux colors and browser toolbar policy
            └─ login mode + emergency lock configuration`} label="Omarchy consumes generated tokens; it does not send a theme back into Lucent" />
    <p>Choose Dark or Light in Lucent. The component updates its palette, persists the preference, then invokes <code>ThemePort</code> on the ordered effect worker. <code>LucentTheme</code> runs the export adapter. The standard Omarchy theme picker is replaced, and arbitrary stock theme names are rejected while the integration is active.</p>
    <p>The exporter calls the installed template generator with <code>OMARCHY_THEME_HEADLESS=1</code> and <code>OMARCHY_THEME_SKIP_BACKGROUND=1</code>. It does not run theme hooks or a reverse synchronization loop. Application support differs: GTK applications may override named colors; browsers may need a reload/restart for managed toolbar colors; websites and app-specific themes remain their own content.</p>
    <p>Exports have a first-run backup and managed CSS blocks. Failures appear as <code>theme_error</code> in inspection and in the theme panel. The native palette can change before an external application finishes applying its export. See <a href={withBase('/design-system/')}>the token reference</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('scripts/lucent-theme.py')}>the export adapter</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="menus"><span class="eyebrow">04 / EXISTING BINDS, NATIVE PRESENTATION</span><h2>Compatibility is an adapter.</h2>
    <p>The menu bridge reads installed JSONC definitions and user extensions. The reusable Rust picker accepts entries, labels and opaque values; it does not execute commands. Python integration adapters discover system state and validate the exact selected value before invoking an argument array. Trusted user menu actions may still be shell expressions.</p>
    <p><code>lucent-compat.py</code> translates known stock IPC names to native actions. For example, <code>omarchy-shell shell toggle omarchy.audio</code> opens the native audio selector, and notification dismissal targets Lucent’s notification port. Unknown operations return an error. User extensions can intentionally launch other applications and remain user-owned.</p>
    <p>These Python scripts are distribution compatibility adapters, not framework APIs. The domain has typed ports for capabilities used by Rust components; the generic framework has no knowledge of Omarchy names, palette export files or system commands. Read <a href={withBase('/docs/domain/')}>the ports and adapters guide</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify"><span class="eyebrow">05 / CHECK AND RECOVER</span><h2>Inspect the running owners.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code="systemctl --user status lucent.service lucent-polkit.service lucent-idle.service lucent-clipboard.service\nlucent-cli inspect\nhyprctl -j layers\n\n# Explicit return to the original desktop integration\npython3 scripts/lucent-setup.py rollback" label="Inside the graphical guest session" />
    <p>Rollback removes only managed blocks, restores the application theme backup, stops native desktop services and starts the installed stock shell. Native binaries and saved preferences remain. Login-manager rollback is separate. Review startup and command contracts after distribution updates; keep local process dumps, credentials and private screenshots out of Git.</p>
  </section>
</DocGuide>
